# Engine telemetry — API.md §`GET /api/v1/telemetry`
#
# The whole engine state as JSON in one call: ruleset, lifetime counters,
# per-stage latency totals, upstreams, cache and memory. Replaces polling
# /cache and /debug/memory separately.
#
# Stable contract: new fields may be added, existing ones never change meaning
# or units. Figures that may change with the implementation live under
# /debug/* instead — see the boundary below.

@base = {{host}}/api/v1
@auth = Authorization: Bearer {{apiKey}}

### Everything. Blocks: process, ruleset, counters, latency, upstreams, cache,
### memory.
GET {{base}}/telemetry
{{auth}}

### Auth is required, like every /api/v1 route — expect 401.
GET {{base}}/telemetry

# ─── Reading the response ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# EVERY counter is process-lifetime cumulative. Charting a rate means deltaing
# two reads — and checking `process.uptime_seconds` first, because a restart
# returns all of them to zero and a delta across that boundary is meaningless,
# not merely small.
#
# JSON has no equivalent of Prometheus's `# TYPE`. The one field that is a
# LAST-VALUE GAUGE rather than a total is:
#   counters.cache_cleanup.last_duration_micros
# Deltaing it produces nonsense.
#
# `latency` gives `count` and `sum_seconds`, never an average: a lifetime mean
# flattens within hours of uptime. Delta both and divide for the interval mean.
# Percentiles need buckets — `history.http` serves them, windowed.
#
# counters.dns.cache_hits + cache_misses == pass + allow, never + block: a
# blocked query never reaches the cache (ADR-0001). Divide a hit ratio by the
# resolved queries, not by every query, or the ratio falls as the blocker
# improves.
#
# counters.dns.answers counts what the CLIENT saw, on its own axis:
#   servfail_synthesized  every upstream failed and no stale entry covered it,
#                         so FastAdHunter minted the SERVFAIL itself
#   servfail_relayed      an upstream answered SERVFAIL; passed through
#   refused_relayed       an upstream answered REFUSED; passed through
# A PARALLEL AXIS, NOT A PARTITION: each of those queries is also counted under
# pass/allow and under cache_misses, so the identity above still holds. Adding
# the three to pass+allow+block double-counts.
#
# A stale serve that masked an upstream failure is NOT here — the client got
# records, so it is counted by cache_stale. Reading these three as "outage
# minutes" therefore understates the outage and overstates the harm: that is
# the point of the split.
#
# Per-interval history of the same three figures is history.http's
# `fields=answers_delta`; there is no per-query outcome on any endpoint.
#
# counters.http is the proxy's own tally, separate from counters.dns because
# "queries" has meant DNS questions since p1-08:
#   pass / allow / block  the URL verdict; sum = requests the proxy served
#   response_bytes        relayed downstream — makes "a block ships nothing"
#                         a trend rather than an assertion
#   refused_claim         the request line itself: a Host that is missing,
#                         duplicated, malformed, or a bare IP
#                         ([egress] allow_ip_literal_hosts). All four causes
#                         land on this one figure
#   refused_destination   the RESOLVED address fell outside [egress]
#                         allow_destinations — a public name pointing at
#                         192.168.x.x is refused here, not by DNS
# NOT A PARTITION of pass+allow+block, and not symmetric either. refused_claim
# is taken before the verdict, so it is outside that sum. refused_destination
# is NOT: the egress check runs after the verdict, and the request was already
# emitted as pass (status 403, zero bytes). Reading pass as "requests served"
# overstates it by the number of destination refusals. Neither counter
# says who or why. refused_claim mixes a broken client (missing, duplicated or
# malformed Host) with a probe (bare IP); refused_destination mixes a rebind
# with one of your own devices the proxy is breaking. Refusals log at `debug`,
# so these two are the only standing record that a refusal happened at all —
# the cause is in the log line's `reason` field, not in the counter.
#
# counters.dns_tcp_connections is the DNS-over-TCP listener's bound in numbers:
#   active           GAUGE — connections open right now
#   peak             process-lifetime high-water mark of `active`; the figure
#                    that sizes [dns] tcp_max_connections after a soak
#   closed_oversize  connections closed because the client's length prefix
#                    exceeded the internal 16 KiB message bound; non-zero on a
#                    household LAN is a misbehaving client, not a limit to raise
# DoT is NOT in this group. Size [dns] tcp_max_connections from this one alone;
# adding the two sizes a ceiling from traffic it does not bound.
#
# counters.dns_dot_connections is the same three figures for DoT, on
# [dns.listen] dot_port, in a gauge of its own — one number for two transports
# could not answer a question about either. Same meanings, same 16 KiB bound.
# A connection is counted WHEN IT IS ACCEPTED, before the TLS handshake
# completes: a client stalling in a handshake already holds one of the 64
# slots, so it belongs in the figure that says whether 64 is enough. The cap is
# compiled in (fah_dns::DOT_MAX_CONNECTIONS) and has no config key; `peak` at
# 64 means it was reached and the next client waited in the kernel backlog.
#
# counters.dns_udp_inflight is the UDP admission guard ([dns] udp_max_inflight):
#   active           GAUGE — UDP queries in flight right now
#   peak             process-lifetime high-water mark of `active`
#   shed             datagrams dropped unanswered because the ceiling was full
# All zero while the key is 0 (the default): no counter touched, no counting.
# All three connection groups are pushed on the same 10 s poll as
# ruleset/upstreams — up to one interval old.
#
# counters.tasks_died is the supervisor's tally: long-lived tasks (rules
# scheduler, event fan-out, perf sampler, telemetry poll, policy ticker, SWR
# workers, cache cleanup, the two stats schedulers, and the HTTP, HTTPS and API
# acceptors) that ended before shutdown. Checked every 10 s; each death is
# logged ONCE at `error` with the task name and cause (panic message, or
# "returned"), then counted here. Report-only: nothing restarts the task and the
# process does not exit — the resolver keeps answering while that task's work
# stays stopped until the container is restarted. Normal value 0. Non-zero: read
# the engine log, then restart.
# /health does NOT change. A DNS listener dying is a different path and exits.
#
# The log line's task name says what you lost:
#   HTTP acceptor    port 8080 stopped accepting; no proxying, no URL filtering
#   HTTPS acceptor   port 8444's proxy leg stopped; no SNI filtering
#   API acceptor     this endpoint, the dashboard and DoH are gone with it —
#                    which is why a dead API acceptor is visible only in the log
# DNS on 53 and DoT on 853 keep answering in all three cases. That is the whole
# point of the split: a dead dashboard must not take the resolver with it.

# ─── The producer boundary ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# What lands here vs on /debug/* is decided by WHO PRODUCES a figure, not by
# how useful it looks:
#
#   FastAdHunter's own counters, ruleset, latency, upstreams, cache → here
#   Kernel: process_rss, process_peak_rss, major/minor page faults    → here
#   The linked allocator: allocator_committed_*                       → /debug
#
# The kernel figures come from getrusage and stay readable whatever allocator
# is linked in, which is why the stable surface may promise them.

# The allocator fields live in debug.http; the cache block is also served
# standalone by cache.http.
